  --------------------------  try-with-resources Cases (Java 7+, enhanced Java 9+) - MISSING, added for Java 17  -------------------------------

    // case 34   try-with-resources alone, no catch/finally - VALID (unlike plain "try" in case 13)
        try(FileReader fr = new FileReader("a.txt")){
            System.out.println("hello");
        }
        *** difference from case 13 :- plain try REQUIRES catch or finally, but try-with-resources
            does NOT - the resource's close() being called automatically satisfies the compiler,
            though FileNotFoundException from "new FileReader(...)" itself still needs handling/declaring


    // case 35   try-with-resources + catch + finally - VALID
        try(FileReader fr = new FileReader("a.txt")){
            stmt 1;
        }
        catch(IOException e){
            stmt 2;
        }
        finally{
            stmt 3;
        }
        *** order of closing :- fr.close() runs BEFORE catch/finally blocks execute
            (resource closing happens as part of the try block's own completion)


    // case 36   try-with-resources, resource not effectively final - IN-VALID
        FileReader fr = new FileReader("a.txt");
        fr = new FileReader("b.txt");     // fr reassigned - no longer effectively final
        try(fr){
            stmt 1;
        }
        Compile Time Error :- variable fr might not be effectively final (Java 9+ resource-reuse rule)


    // case 37   multiple resources in try-with-resources - VALID
        try(FileReader fr = new FileReader("a.txt");
            BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(fr)){
            stmt 1;
        }
        *** closing order :- REVERSE of declaration - br.close() first, then fr.close()


    // case 38   resource class not implementing AutoCloseable - IN-VALID
        class MyResource { void close(){ } }   // does NOT implement AutoCloseable/Closeable
        try(MyResource m = new MyResource()){
            stmt 1;
        }
        Compile Time Error :- incompatible types - MyResource cannot be converted to AutoCloseable


  --------------------------  Pattern Matching instanceof Case (Java 16+, standard in 17) - MISSING  -------------------------------

    // case 39   catch block using pattern matching on getCause() - VALID
        try{
            stmt 1;
        }
        catch(Exception e){
            if(e.getCause() instanceof IOException io){    // no explicit cast, "io" auto-bound
                System.out.println(io.getMessage());
            }
        }
        *** this is unrelated to catch-matching rules above (case 5-7) - pattern matching here is
            just inspecting the exception's cause chain, not an alternative catch mechanism


📝 QUICK SUMMARY - JAVA 17 ADDITIONS TO TRY/CATCH/FINALLY CASE RULES

| New Case                              | Validity  | Key rule                                                |
|-----------------------------------------|-----------|-------------------------------------------------------------|
| Multi-catch (X \| Y)                    | VALID     | X and Y must be unrelated (no parent-child)                 |
| Multi-catch with related types          | IN-VALID  | Compile error - already caught by subclass relation          |
| try-with-resources alone                | VALID     | No catch/finally required, unlike plain try                  |
| try-with-resources + non-final resource | IN-VALID  | Resource var must be final/effectively final (Java 9+ rule)  |
| try-with-resources, non-AutoCloseable   | IN-VALID  | Resource class must implement AutoCloseable/Closeable         |
| Multiple resources in one try()         | VALID     | Closed in REVERSE order of declaration                        |
| Pattern matching instanceof in catch    | VALID     | Safe inspection of getCause(), no cast needed                 |


*** DEPRECATED >> nothing structurally deprecated within try/catch/finally syntax itself in Java 17.
    Related deprecated items from earlier notes (Thread.stop(Throwable), Object.finalize(),
    SecurityManager-based checked exceptions) still apply if they appear INSIDE these blocks,
    but the try/catch/finally/throw/throws grammar itself is unchanged and fully supported.